<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abraham</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abraham, originally Abram, is a foundational figure revered in the Abrahamic religions: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> patriarch, who began the covenant between the Jewish people and God; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the forebear of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>; and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> a link in the chain of prophets from Adam to Muhammad. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, God called him to leave the house of his father Terah and settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, promised to him and to his descendants through his son Isaac. Nearly all scholars view the patriarchal age as a late literary construct; many believe the traditions developed orally before being written into Genesis in the Persian period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham.jpg" alt="Abraham" width="330" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guercino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guercino_Abramo_ripudia_Agar_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ur is possibly the 'Ur of the Chaldees' that the Book of Genesis names as the birthplace of <b>Abraham</b>, although Şanlıurfa, Urkesh, Urartu and Kutha have also been proposed, and the Chaldeans did not settle nearby until about 850 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bahá’í Faith teaches that there is one God, whose essence is unknowable, who has revealed himself to humanity through a series of messengers, called Manifestations of God: among them <b>Abraham</b>, Moses, Zoroaster, Krishna, the Buddha, Jesus, Muhammad, the Báb and Bahá’u’lláh.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad called the people of Mecca to worship the one God, Allah, the God of <b>Abraham</b>, to abandon idols, to care for the poor and to prepare for the Day of Judgement.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the biblical narrative, God made a covenant with <b>Abraham</b>, promising him descendants and a land; his grandson Jacob, renamed Israel, was the father of twelve tribes; the Israelites, enslaved in Egypt, were freed under the leadership of Moses, received the Torah from God at Mount Sinai and…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The narrative begins with creation and the patriarchs <b>Abraham</b>, Isaac and Jacob, whose children become the ancestors of the Twelve Tribes.</blockquote><a href="/work/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Moses is named 136 times, and <b>Abraham</b> appears in 35 chapters.</blockquote><a href="/work/quran">Quran · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
